**CLAIM 1 — CONFIRMED.** `F_X` is `∂F/∂X` in `k[X,Y,Z]`. Chain rule gives `F_X=Z^{d-1}f_x(X/Z,Y/Z)`, so the 2×2 is the degree-`(d+e−2)` homogenization of `J`. At `Z=1` it is `j`; a form of that degree with constant dehomogenization is uniquely `jZ^{d+e−2}` (each `(i,j)` has one complementary `Z`-power, no cancellation). Explicit check: `g=y+x^2`, `f=x+g^2` produces `F_XG_Y−F_YG_X=Z^4`, including the padded `Z^3` in `F_X`.

**CLAIM 2 — CONFIRMED (a)–(e).** `J=d(e-1)x^{d-1}y^{e-2}-ex^{e-1}` matches (5.12) and is non-constant. `P(X)-v` is linear in `v`, hence irreducible, so `[k(u)(x):k(u)(P(x))]=\deg_x P=d(e-1)` (lead term `(-1)^{e-1}x^{d(e-1)}`, and `d(e-1)>e`). Energy arithmetic is exact: `Bα(Bβ-1)/(αβ)=B^2-B/β`. Sample: `(α,β,B)=(2,3,2)` gives `td=20`, `E=10/3`. The family has common leading power `X^{Bα},X^{Bβ}` (so even `J(F_d,G_e)=0`) but fails (4.1): `(2,3,1)` gives `4XYZ-3X^2Z\neq cZ^3`.

**Strategic.** Yes. Finite generation, rooftop convexity, Hodge/AF (`E_{MR}≥0`, wrong sign), and common-leading-power do not produce a `B`-uniform ceiling; they hold for this family with `E_{MR}=B^2-B/β→∞`. A G5 `td`-ceiling must use the full identity (4.1), not its leading-form shadow.
